eason":2025,"team":"CHI","distance":0.7927,"shared":22,"play_caller":"Dennis Allen"},{"season":2022,"team":"LV","distance":0.8295,"shared":22,"play_caller":"Patrick Graham"}]},"overview":{"overview":"The Saints went 5-12 in 2024 and were outscored 398 to 338. Neither side held up, as the offense ranked 25th in EPA per play and the defense 24th in EPA allowed. Their most distinctive traits were the fewest receivers on the field and the 2nd lowest Cover 3 rate in the league.","offense":"The Saints offense was well below average by EPA per play, and it was much better running the ball than passing it. Their run and pass mix stayed close to what game situations called for. Schematically, they stood out for the fewest receivers on the field and the 2nd lowest 11 personnel usage in the league. Derek Carr ran the passing game, with Alvin Kamara leading the team in targets.","defense":"The Saints defense was well below average by EPA allowed per play. It was much better against the pass than the run, ranking 14th and 30th. Carl Granderson was as productive as expected, but the bigger surprise was Chase Young, who had 5.5 sacks, after not being a regular on this defense last season. Schematically, they had the 2nd lowest Cover 3 rate in the league.","special":"The Saints were about average in how often they went for it on fourth down. Their kickers made 87% of field goals, which was well above average. On the roster side, they put an unusually large share of their salary cap into tight ends, about 9% of the money on the books.","players":"Derek Carr played nearly every snap at quarterback and threw for 2,145 yards. Targets were spread around, with Alvin Kamara and Juwan Johnson leading a group where nobody took more than a fifth of the looks. Alvin Kamara handled most of the work in the backfield with 60% of the carries. On defense, Bryan Bresee led the team with 7.5 sacks and Tyrann Mathieu picked off 3 passes.","coaching":"Klint Kubiak called the offense and Dennis Allen called the defense. Their offense looked most like the 2025 Seahawks under Klint Kubiak and the 2025 Ravens under Todd Monken. Their defense looked most like the 2020 Lions and the 2022 Cowboys under Dan Quinn.","trends":"The offense went from 13th in EPA per play in 2023 to 25th in 2024, a big step back. The defense moved from 5th in EPA allowed per play in 2023 to 24th in 2024, a big step back. The biggest change in approach was number of tight ends on the field, which went from 1.09 to 1.6.","notes":"Every data source is available for 2024. This season uses the newer formation labels, which group singleback, I formation and empty sets together as under center, so those older formation rates are not shown."}}